  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Hydnum subolympicum is a small, delicate tooth fungus belonging to the Hydnaceae family, characterized by its pale coloring and diminutive spines beneath the cap. This species is found in New England forests and represents a fascinating example of the diversity within the tooth fungus group.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Spines Instead of Gills",
      "description": "Like all Hydnum species, this fungus has tooth-like spines running down the underside of the cap rather than traditional gills, which is the primary distinguishing feature of the family.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small, Pale Cap",
      "description": "The fruiting body is notably small and delicate with a whitish to pale tan cap, making it easy to overlook in the forest litter.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Brittle Texture",
      "description": "The flesh is characteristically fragile and brittle, breaking easily when handled or disturbed.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Hydnum subolympicum favors moist, well-established hardwood and mixed forests in New England, typically fruiting in late summer and fall among leaf litter and mossy areas.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The tooth-like spines of Hydnum species are actually modified gills that represent an evolutionary adaptation for spore dispersal, functioning just as efficiently as traditional gills despite their unusual appearance.",
